    94	## 2. Route 1 — Chart-Jacobian / Newton–Puiseux
    95	
    96	### 2.1 The two charts are the same chart; the transformation law
    97	
    98	The registry's Route 1 asks for the transformation law relating Chau's
    99	unresolved-chart order to Żołądek's resolved N–P order. It is an identity, not
   100	an estimate, and the two charts coincide after a dictionary.
   101	
   102	> **Proposition 2.1 (chart dictionary).** Chau's `Φ(t,ξ)=(t^{-m_φ},
   103	> φ(t^{-m_φ},ξ))` is Żołądek's alteration chart `θ~` with the roles of the two
   104	> coordinates exchanged and
   105	> `(k, l, v, u) = (m_φ, n_φ - m_φ, t, ξ)`.
   106	> Consequently `l - k = n_φ - 2m_φ` and the two multiplicity formulas
   107	> `mu_D = l-k` [Z-6.7] and `ord_t det DF_φ = n_φ-2m_φ-1` [C-3.1] are one
   108	> statement.
   109	>
   110	> *Proof.* Write Chau's series as `y = ⋯ + ξ x^{γ}` with `x=t^{-m_φ}`, so the
   111	> `ξ`-term is `ξ t^{-m_φ γ}`. Then `∂x/∂ξ = 0`, `∂x/∂t = -m_φ t^{-m_φ-1}`,
   112	> `∂y/∂ξ = t^{-m_φγ}`, hence `det DΦ = -m_φ t^{-m_φ-m_φγ-1}`. Comparing with
   113	> Chau's printed `-m_φ t^{n_φ-2m_φ-1}` gives `-m_φγ = n_φ - m_φ`, i.e. the
   114	> `ξ`-coefficient sits in `t`-degree `n_φ-m_φ`. Żołądek's `θ~` has `y=v^{-k}`
   115	> and `u`-coefficient in `v`-degree `l`, and `Jac θ~ = -k v^{l-k-1}`. Matching
   116	> `t ↔ v`, `ξ ↔ u`, `m_φ ↔ k`, `n_φ-m_φ ↔ l` identifies the charts and the
   117	> Jacobian orders. ∎
   118	
   119	### 2.2 What the Keller condition buys, exactly
   120	
   121	> **Proposition 2.2 (chart form of `mu_l=1`).** Let `l` be a map-dicritical of
   122	> the Keller map `F` with **affine** image, in the chart `Φ` of Prop. 2.1.
   123	> Then `F_φ := F∘Φ` is holomorphic across `{t=0}` ([C-3.6ii]: `a_φ=b_φ=0`) and
   124	> ```
   125	> det DF_φ(t,ξ) = -m_φ J · t^{mu_l - 1},        mu_l = n_φ - 2m_φ = l - k.
   126	> ```
   127	> In particular (i) `mu_l >= 1` is *forced* by holomorphy of `F_φ`, with no
   128	> further input; and (ii) `mu_l = 1` holds **iff** `det DF_φ ≡ -m_φ J`, a
   129	> nonzero constant — i.e. iff the chart map `F_φ` is itself a Keller germ along
   130	> `{t=0}`, hence a local biholomorphism at every point of `{t=0}`.
   131	>
   132	> *Proof.* [C-3.1] gives `det DF_φ = J·det DΦ = -m_φ J t^{n_φ-2m_φ-1}` with `J`
   133	> a nonzero constant. At a generic point of `{t=0}` choose target coordinates
   134	> `(w,τ)` with `D_l = {τ=0}`: since `Phi|_l` is non-constant, `w∘F_φ` has
   135	> non-vanishing `ξ`-derivative there, and by definition of the generic local
   136	> degree `τ∘F_φ = t^{mu_l}·(unit)`. Expanding the wedge,
   137	> `ord_t det DF_φ = mu_l - 1`. Comparing exponents gives `mu_l = n_φ-2m_φ`,
   138	> which is `l-k` by Prop. 2.1, recovering [Z-6.7]. Holomorphy of `F_φ` forces
   139	> `ord_t det DF_φ >= 0`, i.e. `mu_l >= 1`. If `mu_l = 1` the Jacobian is the
   140	> nonvanishing constant `-m_φ J`, so `F_φ` is a local biholomorphism along
   141	> `{t=0}`; conversely a nonvanishing Jacobian gives `ord = 0`, i.e. `mu_l=1`. ∎
   142	
   143	Proposition 2.2 is the exact chart-level form of promoted Lemma 4.2
