+otherwise.  For every normalization place over \(p\), \(l_1'\) supplies
+one boundary point of local multiplicity one and \(l_2'\) one of local
+multiplicity two, except that the marked \(l_2'\)-point has multiplicity
+three.  The two physical points belonging to a fixed normalization place
+are distinct: otherwise one local multiplicity would have to be both one
+and two (or three).  There are no separate \(L_C\) “epsilon” points in
+the repaired finite model; the contrary term in the printed \(N=4\)
